    "explanation": "An order (Latin: ordo) is one of the eight major hierarchical ranks in Linnaean taxonomy, placed between family and class. Orders group organisms that share ancestry; for example, all owls belong to the order Strigiformes. A superorder may be placed above an order and a suborder directly beneath it.\n\nWhich organisms belong in an order is decided by taxonomists, and there are no hard rules, so experts often disagree and some orders are recognized more widely than others. Order names are usually capitalized and follow naming patterns in some groups: plants, fungi and algae end in -ales; birds and fishes in -iformes; many insects in -ptera, while other groups are less consistent.",
